<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Chongyang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1113–<a href="/years/1170">1170</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Wang Zhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Chongyang, born Wang Zhe, was a Daoist teacher of the Jin dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and the founder of the Quanzhen (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school">Complete Perfection</a>) School. After failing in an official career, he said he had met immortals in 1159 and lived for years as a hermit. In 1167 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, where he gathered seven disciples, the Seven Perfected, who spread his teaching of monastic discipline and inner cultivation across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-chongyang.jpg" alt="Wang Chongyang" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phoenix Darshan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Chongyang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
